WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.660
How long can you hold your&nbsp;
breath underwater? Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.520
it won't be anywhere nearly as long as&nbsp;
Tom Cruise can — or the cast of Avatar.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.980
During a presser for Avatar: The Way of Water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.360
Kate Winslet talked at some length about how&nbsp;
long she and her castmates held their breath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.820
while filming underwater. Reportedly, Winslet&nbsp;
herself held her breath for seven minutes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.380
fifteen seconds on set, demolishing a&nbsp;
record previously held by Tom Cruise.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.080
While filming Mission: Impossible — Rogue Nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.160
Cruise was said to have held his breath&nbsp;
for six minutes to film an underwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.180
sequence. In a behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
featurette, Cruise said of the stunt:

00:00:36.446 --> 00:00:39.240
"I had to prove to everyone&nbsp;
that it was actually safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.080
and spend time with the safety guys and&nbsp;
the safety officers, and show them."

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.540
After surpassing Cruise by over a minute,&nbsp;
Winslet very much seemed to panic — in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.500
the actor momentarily thought she&nbsp;
was dead. Director James Cameron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.000
was so stunned by Winslet's time that he&nbsp;
even seemed jealous. He told the press:

00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:07.980
Though Winslet may hold Avatar's best&nbsp;
time underwater, one of her co-stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.240
could also have crushed Cruise's record.&nbsp;
Cameron estimated that Winslet outdid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.080
even the film's diving trainers, but&nbsp;
he also noted that Sigourney Weaver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.400
came "very close" to her co-star's time&nbsp;
underwater. Winslet jumped on the point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.220
eagerly explaining that the 73-year-old Hollywood&nbsp;
icon may have overtaken Tom Cruise's time as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.020
well. Suggesting that Weaver's time may have&nbsp;
come to about "six-thirty," Winslet said:

00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:43.500
For her part, the Alien star&nbsp;
seemed unbothered by whether or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.220
not she exceeded Cruise's Mission&nbsp;
Impossible benchmark. She joked:

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:54.300
Winslet went on to briefly recount her&nbsp;
own record-breaking underwater experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.440
stating that the first thing she said&nbsp;
upon resurfacing was that they needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.200
radio the set — basically, she wanted Cameron to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.060
know immediately that she had spent over&nbsp;
seven minutes underwater. Winslet added:

00:02:08.243 --> 00:02:13.380
While having to perform underwater for extended&nbsp;
periods made Avatar: The Way of Water a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.540
acting challenge for all involved, for Sigourney&nbsp;
Weaver specifically, the hurdles didn't end with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.400
holding her breath. Her new character, Kiri, is&nbsp;
far different from her previous role in the Avatar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.340
franchise, Dr. Grace Augustine, who was killed&nbsp;
off in the 2009 original. More specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.080
she's only a kid, meaning Weaver had to&nbsp;
totally alter her usual acting routine.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.100
During an interview with Empire, Weaver&nbsp;
touched on what it was like portraying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.300
Na'vi teenager and the difficulties that&nbsp;
came with such a character. She said:

00:02:47.851 --> 00:02:50.340
Nevertheless, she took the&nbsp;
task of playing a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.220
as young as Kiri as a welcome challenge. She adds:

